<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Decembrists</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Decembrist revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Decembrists were Russian army officers and nobles who staged a revolt in St Petersburg on 14 December 1825 (Old Style), refusing to swear allegiance to Nicholas I and seeking a constitution or a republic and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. The rising, and a second in the south, was quickly crushed; five leaders were hanged and over a hundred exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Later Russian liberals and revolutionaries honoured them as martyrs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/decembrists.jpg" alt="Decembrists" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vasily Timm · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_Wilhelm_Timm_-_Dekabristen-Aufstand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Many Russian officers were encouraged by the early liberal reforms of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, which Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-speransky">Mikhail Speransky</a> advanced until his exile in 1812. The tsar later turned back to restraint and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>, after unrest he believed came from liberalisation. Officers who had served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and had seen the suffering of peasant soldiers, were drawn to reform. They formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a>, among them the Union of Salvation and the Union of Prosperity, and later the Northern Society in the capital and the Southern Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Pavel Pestel recorded that the nobility, who were to petition the emperor to free the serfs, could not be persuaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> died on 1 December 1825 (19 November Old Style). His brother Konstantin had privately renounced the throne two years earlier, but this was not generally known, and the guards swore allegiance to him. When Nicholas stepped forward, the Northern Society tried to persuade regiments not to swear to him, and elected Prince Sergei Trubetskoy interim ruler. On 26 December (14 December Old Style) about 3,000 men under their officers assembled in Senate Square and refused the oath to Nicholas I, proclaiming loyalty to Konstantin. Trubetskoy deserted, and Prince Eugene Obolensky was appointed in his place.</p><p>For hours the rebels stood against some 9,000 loyal troops, while a crowd of onlookers fraternised with them without joining. Count Mikhail Miloradovich, sent by Nicholas to parley, was shot dead by Pyotr Kakhovsky. A squad of grenadiers entered the Winter Palace but failed to seize it, and the revolt was put down. In the south the police had arrested Pestel the day before. Sergey Muravyov-Apostol raised the Chernigov Regiment on 10 January 1826 (29 December Old Style), and the rising was defeated within five days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At the trial members of the Northern and Southern Societies and the United Slavs were judged, and five leaders were sentenced to hang. Many others were sent to prison or exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and the first convicts left for the mines of Nerchinsk on 25 July 1826. Enlisted soldiers received harsher treatment: 178 were sentenced to run the gauntlet and about four thousand were sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Autocracy lasted almost a century more, although Nicholas I turned his attention to the empire's internal problems and set Speransky to codify the laws.</p><p>Historians describe the revolt as the beginning of a revolutionary movement and the first open breach between the government and the reformist nobility. Alexander Herzen put the profiles of the executed on the cover of his periodical Polar Star, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> addressed poems to Decembrist friends. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> began a novel about the movement that grew into War and Peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Decembrist revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Novgorod is the most important example of a republican city-state in medieval Eastern Europe, and its history has often been used, by nineteenth-century liberals and <b>Decembrists</b> as well as later historians, to show that Russian history contained a tradition of self-government before Muscovite autocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Decembrists</b> wanted to replace the autocracy of the Russian Empire with a constitutional order, and from the beginning they considered freeing the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Three emperors ruled in these decades: Alexander I (1801–1825), who drove Napoleon’s army out of Russia in 1812; his brother Nicholas I (1825–1855), who began his reign by crushing the <b>Decembrist revolt</b> of noble officers; and Nicholas’s son Alexander II (1855–1881), who freed the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Cannon fire dispersed them; five leaders were hanged, and the <b>Decembrists</b> inspired later Russian radicals.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
